"TopoPerception: A Shortcut-Free Evaluation of Global Visual Perception in Large Vision-Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11831)\nciteturn6142academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Date: Fri Nov 14 19:45:56 2025 ... Since topology depends on the global structure of an image and is invariant to local features, TopoPerception enables a shortcut-free assessment of global perception, fundamentally distinguishing it from semantically rich tasks.\nTitle: TopoPerception: A Shortcut-Free Evaluation of Global Visual Perception in Large Vision-Language Models\nAuthors: Wenhao Zhou, Hao Zheng, Rong Zhao\nDate: Fri Nov 14 19:45:56 2025\n\nLarge Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) typically align visual features from an encoder with a pre-trained Large Language Model (LLM). However, this makes the visual perception module a bottleneck, which constrains the overall capabilities of LVLMs. Conventional evaluation benchmarks, while rich in visual semantics, often contain unavoidable local shortcuts that can lead to an overestimation of models' perceptual abilities. Here, we introduce TopoPerception, a benchmark that leverages topological properties to rigorously evaluate the global visual perception capabilities of LVLMs across various granularities. Since topology depends on the global structure of an image and is invariant to local features, TopoPerception enables a shortcut-free assessment of global perception, fundamentally distinguishing it from semantically rich tasks. We evaluate state-of-the-art models on TopoPerception and find that even at the coarsest perceptual granularity, all models perform no better than random chance, indicating a profound inability to perceive global visual features. Notably, a consistent trend emerge within model families: more powerful models with stronger reasoning capabilities exhibit lower accuracy. This suggests that merely scaling up models is insufficient to address this deficit and may even exacerbate it. Progress may require new training paradigms or architectures. TopoPerception not only exposes a critical bottleneck in current LVLMs but also offers a lens and direction for improving their global visual perception. The data and code are publicly available at: https://github.com/Wenhao-Zhou/TopoPerception.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaper page - TopoPerception: A Shortcut-Free Evaluation of Global Visual Perception in Large Vision-Language Models (https://ofia-docs.france-ioi.org/huggingface/papers/2511.11831)\nciteturn6142search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; Since topology depends on the global structure of an image and is invariant to local features, TopoPerception enables a shortcut-free assessment of global perception, fundamentally distinguishing it from semantically rich tasks. ... Paper author Paper submitter Nov 19, 2025\n\nPapers\n\narxiv:2511.11831\n\nCopy markdown\n\n# TopoPerception: A Shortcut-Free Evaluation of Global Visual Perception in Large Vision-Language Models\n\nPublished on Nov 14, 2025\n\n· Submitted by Wenhao Zhou on Nov 19, 2025\n\nUpvote 1\n\n  *   * Authors:\n\nImage Wenhao Zhou ,\n\n[Button: Hao Zheng] ,\n\n[Button: Rong Zhao]\n\n## Abstract\n\nLarge Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) typically align visual features from an encoder with a pre-trained Large Language Model (LLM). However, this makes the visual perception module a bottleneck, which constrains the overall capabilities of LVLMs. Conventional evaluation benchmarks, while rich in visual semantics, often contain unavoidable local shortcuts that can lead to an overestimation of models' perceptual abilities. Here, we introduce TopoPerception, a benchmark that leverages topological properties to rigorously evaluate the global visual perception capabilities of LVLMs across various granularities. Since topology depends on the global structure of an image and is invariant to local features, TopoPerception enables a shortcut-free assessment of global perception, fundamentally distinguishing it from semantically rich tasks. We evaluate state-of-the-art models on TopoPerception and find that even at the coarsest perceptual granularity, all models perform no better than random chance, indicating a profound inability to perceive global visual features. Notably, a consistent trend emerge within model families: more powerful models with stronger reasoning capabilities exhibit lower accuracy. This suggests that merely scaling up models is insufficient to address this deficit and may even exacerbate it. Progress may require new training paradigms or architectures. TopoPerception not only exposes a critical bottleneck in current LVLMs but also offers a lens and direction for improving their global visual perception. The data and code are publicly available at: https://github.com/Wenhao-Zhou/TopoPerception.\n\nView arXiv page View PDF GitHub 3 Add to collection\n\n### Community\n\nImage\n\nWenhao-Zhou\n\nPaper author Paper submitter Nov 19, 2025\n\nLarge Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) typically align visual features from an encoder with a pre-trained Large Language Model (LLM). However, this makes the visual perception module a bottleneck, which constrains the overall capabilities of LVLMs. Conventional evaluation benchmarks, while rich in visual semantics, often contain unavoidable local shortcuts that can lead to an overestimation of models' perceptual abilities. Here, we introduce TopoPerception, a benchmark that leverages topological properties to rigorously evaluate the global visual perception capabilities of LVLMs across various granularities. Since topology depends on the global structure of an image and is invariant to local features, TopoPerception enables a shortcut-free assessment of global perception, fundamentally distinguishing it from semantically rich tasks. We evaluate state-of-the-art models on TopoPerception and find that even at the coarsest perceptual granularity, all models perform no better than random chance, indicating a profound inability to perceive global visual features. Notably, a consistent trend emerge within model families: more powerful models with stronger reasoning capabilities exhibit lower accuracy. This suggests that merely scaling up models is insufficient to address this deficit and may even exacerbate it. Progress may require new training paradigms or architectures. TopoPerception not only exposes a critical bottleneck in current LVLMs but also offers a lens and direction for improving their global visual perception.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTopoPerception: 对大型视觉语言模型全局视觉感知的无捷径评估 | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/zh/abs/2511.11831)\nciteturn6142search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Submitted 14 Nov 2025 ... @misc{zhou2025topoperceptionshortcutfreeevaluationglobal, title={TopoPerception: A Shortcut-Free Evaluation of Global Visual Perception in Large Vision-Language Models}, author={Wenhao Zhou and Hao Zheng and Rong Zhao}, year={2025}, eprint={2511.11831}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.AI}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11831}, }\nSubmitted 14 Nov 2025\n\nzh\n\n# TopoPerception: 对大型视觉语言模型全局视觉感知的无捷径评估\n\nImageTsinghua UniversityImageMITWenhao Zhou\n\nHZ\n\nHao Zheng\n\nRZ\n\nRong Zhao\n\n## Abstract\n\n**大视觉-语言模型（LVLMs）通常将来自编码器的视觉特征与预训练的大型语言模型（LLM）对齐。然而，这使得视觉感知模块成为瓶颈，从而限制了LVLMs的整体能力。传统的评估基准尽管在视觉语义上很丰富，却常包含无法避免的局部捷径，这可能导致对模型感知能力的过高估计。** **在本文中，我们引入了TopoPerception，一个利用拓扑特性来严谨评估LVLMs在不同粒度下的全局视觉感知能力的基准。由于拓扑结构依赖于图像的全局结构且对局部特征具有不变性，TopoPerception能够实现对全局感知的无捷径评估，从根本上将其与语义丰富的任务区分开来。我们在TopoPerception上评估了最先进的模型，发现即使在最粗的感知粒度下，所有模型的表现都不比随机猜测好，这表明它们在感知全局视觉特征方面存在深刻的不足。## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{zhou2025topoperceptionshortcutfreeevaluationglobal, title={TopoPerception: A Shortcut-Free Evaluation of Global Visual Perception in Large Vision-Language Models}, author={Wenhao Zhou and Hao Zheng and Rong Zhao}, year={2025}, eprint={2511.11831}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.AI}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.11831}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTopoPerception: Una Evaluación Sin Atajos de la Percepción Visual Global en Grandes Modelos de Visión-Lenguaje | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/es/abs/2511.11831)\nciteturn6142search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Submitted 14 Nov 2025 ... Image: Comparación de rendimiento entre siete LVLM de última generación en el benchmark TopoPerception, mostrando tasas de precisión que rondan la probabilidad aleatoria (20%) en todos los modelos, con algunas tendencias contraintuitivas donde los modelos más potentes rinden peor.\nSubmitted 14 Nov 2025\n\nes\n\n# TopoPerception: Una Evaluación Sin Atajos de la Percepción Visual Global en Grandes Modelos de Visión-Lenguaje\n\nImageTsinghua UniversityImageMITWenhao Zhou\n\nHZ\n\nHao Zheng\n\nRZ\n\nRong Zhao\n\n## Abstract\n\nAquí tienes la traducción del resumen: **Grandes Modelos de Visión-Lenguaje (LVLMs) suelen alinear características visuales de un codificador con un Modelo de Lenguaje Grande (LLM) pre-entrenado. Sin embargo, esto convierte al módulo de percepción visual en un cuello de botella, lo que restringe las capacidades generales de los LVLMs. Los *benchmarks* de evaluación convencionales, aunque ricos en semántica visual, a menudo contienen atajos locales inevitables que pueden llevar a una sobreestimación de las habilidades perceptivas de los modelos. Aquí, presentamos TopoPerception, un *benchmark* que aprovecha las propiedades topológicas para evaluar rigurosamente las capacidades de percepción visual global de los LVLMs en diversas granularidades. Los datos y el código están disponibles públicamente en: this https URL.**\n\n## Introducción\n\nLos Grandes Modelos de Visión-Lenguaje (LVLM) han demostrado capacidades impresionantes en numerosas tareas multimodales, logrando un alto rendimiento en benchmarks que combinan la percepción visual con la comprensión del lenguaje. Sin embargo, persiste una pregunta crítica: ¿Estos modelos realmente perciben y comprenden la estructura global de la información visual, o se basan en atajos locales y señales semánticas para lograr su rendimiento? \"TopoPerception: Una Evaluación Libre de Atajos de la Percepción Visual Global en Grandes Modelos de Visión-Lenguaje\" introduce un benchmark de diagnóstico diseñado para aislar y evaluar rigurosamente las capacidades de percepción visual global de los LVLM de última generación.\n\nImage: Comparación de rendimiento entre siete LVLM de última generación en el benchmark TopoPerception, mostrando tasas de precisión que rondan la probabilidad aleatoria (20%) en todos los modelos, con algunas tendencias contraintuitivas donde los modelos más potentes rinden peor.\n\nLa investigación revela un preocupante cuello de botella perceptual en las arquitecturas LVLM actuales, donde incluso los modelos más avanzados tienen dificultades para percibir propiedades topológicas básicas que describen la estructura global de las imágenes. Este trabajo desafía suposiciones fundamentales sobre cómo fluye la información visual a través de estos sistemas y proporciona información crucial para el futuro desarrollo de la IA multimodal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n에이전트를 위한 웹 구축: 에이전트-웹 상호작용을 위한 선언적 프레임워크 | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/ko/overview/2511.11287)\nciteturn6142search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; November 14, 2025 ... Sven Schultze, Meike Verena Kietzmann, Nils-Lucas Schönfeld, Ruth Stock-Homburg ... 다름슈타트 공과대학교(Technical University of Darmstadt) 연구원들은 웹사이트가 간단한 HTML 요소를 통해 AI 에이전트에게 자신들의 기능을 명시적으로 선언할 수 있도록 함으로써 이러한 불일치를 해결하는 웹-네이티브 프레임워크인 VOIX(Voice-Operated Interaction eXtension)를 소개합니다.\n\n# Building the Web for Agents: A Declarative Framework for Agent-Web Interaction\n\n에이전트를 위한 웹 구축: 에이전트-웹 상호작용을 위한 선언적 프레임워크\n\nNovember 14, 2025\n\nCopy Markdown\n\nSven Schultze, Meike Verena Kietzmann, Nils-Lucas Schönfeld, Ruth Stock-Homburg\n\n### 요약\n\n다름슈타트 공과대학교에서 개발한 VOIX라는 웹 기반 프레임워크는 웹사이트가 간단하고 선언적인 HTML 요소를 사용하여 AI 에이전트에 기능을 노출할 수 있도록 합니다. 이 시스템은 추론 기반 접근 방식에 비해 현저히 낮은 작업 완료 지연 시간을 달성하며, 개발자 연구를 통해 검증된 복잡한 다중 모드 상호작용을 지원합니다.\n\n다름슈타트 공과대학교에서 개발한 VOIX라는 웹 기반 프레임워크는 웹사이트가 간단하고 선언적인 HTML 요소를 사용하여 AI 에이전트에 기능을 노출할 수 있도록 합니다. 이 시스템은 추론 기반 접근 방식에 비해 현저히 낮은 작업 완료 지연 시간을 달성하며, 개발자 연구를 통해 검증된 복잡한 다중 모드 상호작용을 지원합니다.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nПостроение Веба для Агентов: Декларативный Фреймворк для Агент-Веб Взаимодействия | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/ru/overview/2511.11287)\nciteturn6142search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; November 14, 2025Sven Schultze, Meike Verena Kietzmann, Nils-Lucas Schönfeld, Ruth Stock-Homburg ... Показатели доверия к пониманию системы (5,81/7) и производительности (5,14/7) продемонстрировали, что разработчики быстро освоили концепции VOIX и сочли фреймворк надежным.\n\n# Building the Web for Agents: A Declarative Framework for Agent-Web Interaction\n\nПостроение Веба для Агентов: Декларативный Фреймворк для Агент-Веб Взаимодействия\n\nNovember 14, 2025\n\nSven Schultze, Meike Verena Kietzmann, Nils-Lucas Schönfeld, Ruth Stock-Homburg\n\n### Резюме\n\nВеб-нативная структура под названием VOIX, разработанная Дармштадтским техническим университетом, позволяет веб-сайтам предоставлять свои возможности ИИ-агентам с помощью простых, декларативных HTML-элементов. Система достигает значительно меньших задержек при выполнении задач по сравнению с подходами, основанными на инференсе, и поддерживает сложные мультимодальные взаимодействия, что подтверждено исследованиями с разработчиками.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmotional words and pacing: Write AI voice scripts that sound intentional | Vois (https://pre-release.vois.so/blog/emotional-keywords-pacing)\nciteturn6142search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: last week; November 28, 2025\nBack to BlogTips & Tricks\n\n# Emotional words and pacing: Write AI voice scripts that sound intentional\n\nImage: Vois TeamVois Team\n\n•\n\nNovember 28, 2025\n\n•\n\n5 min read\n\nTLDR:Emotional delivery is a writing and review problem, not a secret keyword trick. Draft spoken-language beats, audition them in Vois, use supported expressions only where they add meaning, and regenerate the exact section that needs work.\n\nThe sentence that reads perfectly on a page can collapse when it is spoken. A reveal arrives too quickly. A warning sounds cheerful. A dense explanation gets no room to breathe.\n\nVois gives you a practical way to catch those problems before your audience does. Write the script in the editor, assign the voice that fits the material, audition the actual take, then revise the words, pacing, or pause placement until the meaning lands. The goal is not to find a magic word that forces a performance. It is to give the listener a clear emotional path.\n\n## Start with the feeling, not a keyword list\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNovember 28, 2025 Advisory Committee - Day-to-day project operations - OpenRefine (https://forum.openrefine.org/t/november-28-2025-advisory-committee/2648)\nciteturn6142search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # November 28, 2025 Advisory Committee ...   * Martin open a PR to update the website with November progress 20251126 update by magdmartin · Pull Request #496 · OpenRefine/openrefine.org · GitHub\n\n# November 28, 2025 Advisory Committee\n\nCommunity Day-to-day project operations\n\nminute-advisory\n\nMartin December 1, 2025, 6:58pm 1\n\n## Attendees\n\n  * Julie Faure Lacroix - Advisory Committee\n  * Jan Ainali - Advisory Committee\n  * Martin Magdinier - Project Manager\n\n## Discussions\n\n### Core Dev Group update\n\n  * Martin provided an update regarding the vote on for the Core Dev Group: Nomination of @abbe98 for the Core Developer Group - #4 by tfmorris\n  * Martin Open a PR to update the goverance Update GOVERNANCE.md by magdmartin · Pull Request #7552 · OpenRefine/OpenRefine · GitHub\n\n### Annual Review\n\n  * Martin shared with the group the template and training material we used last year\n  * Meeting will be scheduled early January\n\n### Fundraising Status\n\n  * Martin open a PR to update the website with November progress 20251126 update by magdmartin · Pull Request #496 · OpenRefine/openrefine.org · GitHub\n  * The group review the fundraising calendar and confirmed next steps\n  * The group did not review in details the progress on the fundraising effort with larger donor.\n  * The group continued to work on the Funding Opportunity: 2025 Sloan Letter of Interest\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAI Voices vs Human Narrators: When to Use Each | Vois (https://pre-release.vois.so/blog/ai-voices-vs-human)\nciteturn6142search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; November 29, 2025\n\n# AI Voices vs Human Narrators: When to Use Each\n\nImage: Vois TeamVois Team\n\n•\n\nNovember 29, 2025\n\n•\n\n6 min read\n\nTLDR:Use Vois when a project needs a consistent, locally produced voice that can be reviewed and revised without a new recording session. Use human talent when the performer, spontaneous response, cultural specificity, or emotional interpretation is part of the work. A mixed approach can be the right choice when the content has both jobs.\n\nThe decision is rarely \"AI or human, forever.\" It is whether the voice helps this audience understand, trust, or feel the particular thing you are making.\n\nVois is a strong fit when the work needs a stable narrator, a local production workflow, and an easy way to revise approved material without recreating a recording session. A human narrator is the better fit when their identity, interpretation, cultural knowledge, or presence is part of the promise. The script, the audience, and the release context should decide.\n\nThat distinction saves a lot of false arguments. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCreating Distinct Character Voices for Audiobooks | Vois (https://pre-release.vois.so/blog/character-voices-audiobooks)\nciteturn6142search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: last month; November 20, 2025 ... Vois gives you one project for the character map: speaker-tagged script, voice assignments, pronunciation notes, generated dialogue, timeline review, mastering, and export.\n\n# Creating Distinct Character Voices for Audiobooks\n\nImage: Vois TeamVois Team\n\n•\n\nNovember 20, 2025\n\n•\n\n6 min read\n\nTLDR:Create distinct character voices using library voices and voice cloning, pacing variation, and consistent tagging. Document each character's voice settings for consistency across chapters.\n\nA narrator can carry the story, but dialogue has to tell listeners who entered the room. When two characters share a scene, vague or drifting voices make an audiobook difficult to follow even if every individual line sounds polished.\n\nVois gives you one project for the character map: speaker-tagged script, voice assignments, pronunciation notes, generated dialogue, timeline review, mastering, and export. That means a decision about Sarah's voice in chapter one can be checked again in chapter twelve instead of relying on memory.\n\nThe objective is not a different novelty voice for every line. It is a small, distinct, sustainable cast that serves the story.\n\n## Start with a character map\n\nList the narrator, major characters, recurring minor characters, and incidental roles before you generate the book. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeveloper and Community Engagement: October and early November 2025 - Community - OpenRefine (https://forum.openrefine.org/t/developer-and-community-engagement-october-and-early-november-2025/2625)\nciteturn6142search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Developer and Community Engagement: October and early November 2025 ... I've written up an issue to describe my initial thoughts here: A process for smoke testing extensions · Issue #7537 · OpenRefine/OpenRefine · GitHub\nOpenRefine\n\n# Developer and Community Engagement: October and early November 2025\n\nCommunity\n\nRory November 11, 2025, 9:43pm 1\n\nHi all, apologies for being a bit delayed on this update post. It’s been a busy time! I’d still like to move my Trello board into GitHub, which I’m hoping to finally make time for later this week. I’ll follow up here once that’s migrated.\n\nHere are some highlights from the past month:\n\n  * Working on the WikiConference NYC talk with Esther. This was ultimately postponed but we will still share what we’ve put together\n  * Birdaro open source training. Most of the material so far has been related to high-level components of open source communities (e.g. project governance models) with different projects giving talks on how their projects work.\n  * I’ve been trying to keep the momentum going on the two goal posts we have related to the project grid header. To that end, I’d like to invite interested parties to collaborate in this thread on a design that includes the requested features.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeveloper and Community Engagement: November/December 2025 - Community - OpenRefine (https://forum.openrefine.org/t/developer-and-community-engagement-november-december-2025/2706)\nciteturn6142search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; # Developer and Community Engagement: November/December 2025 ... You can find this project by looking for \"Developer and Community Engagement Tasks\" under the org-level projects, or by clicking this link: https://github.com/orgs/OpenRefine/projects/12 (I think you need to be part of the organization to see it)\n\n# Developer and Community Engagement: November/December 2025\n\nCommunity\n\nRory January 14, 2026, 10:24pm 1\n\nHi all, and welcome to another recap of what I've been working on and a look at what I plan to work on next.\n\n  * Since my last update, Julie and I finished the \"Creating Community Playbooks\" section of the Birdaro course. I mentioned in my last update that we'd chosen to create a playbook for extension developers. We followed through on that and will be sharing the result with folks in the coming weeks. I think this will be a helpful refresh of the existing extension documentation and I'm looking forward to hearing your feedback!\n  * I tried working on a proposal to resolve the issues described in this thread: Apply operation history from json file not working. My goal was to unblock affected users from upgrading to newer versions of OpenRefine without advertising history application as a supported feature. However, even without my change there has been a lot of work on this area of the application. I'd really appreciate additional user feedback.\n  * I'd wanted to work through Advent of Code challenges in OpenRefine, but like every other year I never made it past the first few days. However, that was enough to inspire me to work on a `reduce` function in GREL.\n  * I also recently moved the Trello board I'd been using to track high-level tasks to GitHub. I've only moved a few pull requests and issues into the project so far but will continue to fill it out with tasks that are not currently captured as GitHub issues. You can find this project by looking for \"Developer and Community Engagement Tasks\" under the org-level projects, or by clicking this link: https://github.com/orgs/OpenRefine/projects/12 (I think you need to be part of the organization to see it)\n\nHere are the two main items I'll be focusing on for the next six weeks:\n\n  * The 3.10 release. Tom helpfully opened Poll - 3.10 testing & new column menu and I'd like to encourage everyone to weigh in!\n  * The extension development playbook from the Birdaro course. There's a little bit of review needed before sharing it around but I expect that should wrap up shortly.\n\nFinally, I want to add here that my contract runs through the end of February. I won't disappear after that, but I'll be focusing on other projects. Please feel free to reach out with any comments, questions, or suggestions for areas of focus.\n\nI hope you're all having a great start to the new year!\n\n2 Likes--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRelease Notes | Amendment (https://www.amendment.app/changelog)\nciteturn6142search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; December 22, 2025 ... November 29, 2025\n\nAmendment on a phone got a dedicated mobile drawer for navigation and a cleaned-up layout. You can now install Amendment as a progressive web app, so it lives on your home screen and opens fast.\n\n### Everything else\n\n  * Bill text versions (you can see which version of a bill you’re reading)\n  * A visual design refactor\n  * A sample-questions patch for the home page\n  * Breadcrumbs on deeper pages\n  * Performance work on the bills listing\n\nDecember 22, 2025\n\n## Roll call votes\n\nOne feature this month is worth more than the rest combined: roll call votes. Every vote, every legislator, tied to the bill.\n\n### Roll call votes\n\nFor bills that make it to a vote, the recorded votes are now on the bill page: how your senator voted, how the whole chamber voted, the margin. This is the data that matters most for accountability, more than who sponsored a bill or what a bill says. It’s what elected officials actually did. We’ve been building toward this since June 2024.\n\n### Calendars on bill pages\n\nEach bill now surfaces its legislative calendar: when it was filed, when it moves, hearings, committee referrals. You can see not just where a bill is, but when it’s been touched.\n\n### A Sources page\n\nA new Sources page spells out where Amendment’s data comes from: which official portals, which public-interest libraries like Cornell’s Legal Information Institute, which civic-data partners like Legiscan. We think you should know where your answers come from before you trust them.\n\n### Inline references\n\nThe old reference drawer is gone. Citations from the AI now appear inline, right where you’re reading them. Less jumping, less hidden context, more trust in the answer.\n\n### Everything else\n\n  * A site-wide visual refresh\n  * Georgia, Ohio, and North Carolina added to the coverage map\n  * A cleaner path from a bill page back to its jurisdiction\n\nNovember 29, 2025\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSunday, November 2, 2025 | 3:00 pm (https://focm.org/docs/2025-2026/Invoke_program_pages_web_25-26.pdf)\nciteturn6142search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; – Jazz Improv Magazine 2022 ... the La Jolla Music Festival, to name a few. ... artistic voice in the classical, world music,\nB I O G R A P H Y\naudiences both onstage and off. In the recording \narena, Assad has released seven solo albums \nand appeared on or had her works performed \non another 34. Her music is represented on \nCedille Records, SONY Masterworks, Nonesuch, \nAdventure Music, Edge, Telarc, NSS Music, GHA, \nand CHANDOS. Her innovative and award-\nwinning VOXploration education series on music \ncreation, songwriting, and improvisation has \nbeen presented throughout the world.  \nThe prolific composer has more than 70 works \nCLARICE ASSAD\n“she energetically bends music to her will and \nreshapes it with fascinating results.” \n– Jazz Improv Magazine 2022 \nto her credit, including numerous commissions \nfor Carnegie Hall, the Chamber Music 